# README format

Canonical example: https://github.com/nasibcode/iphone-duo-prep/blob/main/README.md  
Follow [standard-readme](https://github.com/RichardLitt/standard-readme). New or rewritten READMEs must match this shape.

## Required skeleton

```markdown
# <Title>

[![standard-readme compliant](https://img.shields.io/badge/readme%20style-standard-brightgreen.svg?style=flat-square)](https://github.com/RichardLitt/standard-readme)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg?style=flat-square)](./LICENSE)

<1–3 sentence pitch. Bold the product name. Say what it is and what it is not.>

## Table of Contents

- [Background](#background)
- [Install](#install)
- [Usage](#usage)
- [Features](#features)
- [Repository map](#repository-map)   <!-- omit if tiny -->
- [Docs](#docs)                       <!-- omit if none -->
- [Contributing](#contributing)
- [License](#license)

## Background
## Install
## Usage
## Features
## Repository map
## Docs
## Contributing
## License
```

Include domain sections only when real (e.g. Toolchain, Adapters). Drop empty sections — never leave stubs.

## Voice and layout

- Lead with a crisp pitch; separate “will do” from “will not do” (own section if non-obvious).
- Prefer tables for comparisons, command→when-to-use, and docs indexes.
- Show real commands and short code samples; abridge long output with `…`.
- Link deeper docs (`SETUP.md`, `Docs/…`) instead of dumping everything in the README.
- Package / CLI / product names in bold backticks once in the pitch.
- License last: `[MIT](./LICENSE) © <year> <name>`.
- Badge license must match the repo’s actual LICENSE file.

## Do not

- Marketing fluff, emoji walls, or “Built with ❤️”.
- Giant feature lists with no Install/Usage path to a first win.
- Profile/portfolio mini-READMEs (e.g. `username/username`) — those stay short; this rule is for product/library repos.
